{
  "seam": "C5",
  "name": "manifest-rediscovery-case",
  "mark": {
    "required-green": "e0"
  },
  "kind": "transform",
  "grade": "reference",
  "graded_by": [
    {
      "unit": "U2",
      "test": "core/internal/worker/rediscover_test.go — manifests on disk with live, refused and missing sockets: the core dials exactly the live ones and ends the rest terminated"
    },
    {
      "unit": "U11",
      "test": "harness/rigs/kill-core.sh — SIGKILL the core only; on restart it rediscovers the untouched worker"
    }
  ],
  "description": "What a core restarted after kill -9 does with the manifests it finds. Liveness is the dial: a published socket that accepts a connection is live; one that refuses, or is missing (absent from `sockets`), is dead. Never a pid, which means nothing outside the writer's container: fixture-worker-2 writes pid 1, alive in every namespace, and is dead; fixture-sup-2 shares fixture-sup-1's pid and is dead. Every supervisor whose control socket connects is addressed (they are a set). For each connection whose control.sock connects, the core dials it and each session socket that connects — authenticated by SO_PEERCRED alone, with no token and no catch-up of missed bytes at this tag. A session whose socket does not connect (fixture-session-b), and every session of a connection whose control socket does not (fixture-session-c, fixture-session-d — a session socket that still answers does not revive a dead connection), goes down the dead-peer path: a worker the core cannot reach is a dead worker and its sessions are terminated (immortality §3) — worker_lost in the audit, a terminated marker in the recording. The rule is executable in tsbc (_binfmt.rediscover); this case is its record.",
  "corpus": [
    "immortality §2.1",
    "immortality §3",
    "immortality §6.1",
    "decision 5.13",
    "decision 5.30",
    "decision 5.44",
    "phase-00 (rediscovery)"
  ],
  "case": {
